so can you tell us a little bit about  this new technology and how it actually  interfaces with the standard prosthetic  limb so we bring in the standard  prosthetic limb and because sensation  hasn't been available most of them don't  have sensors on them and so we basically  have added this lab system on the top  where we can record or sense how much in  pressure a person is putting on an  object when they grab hold of it and  then that is recorded on a computer  converted into a neural code if you will  a neural stimulation and then we run  that through a set of wires that go in  in the upper arm and then underneath the  skin they go to a cuff electrode that's  then placed around each of the nerves in  the in one of our subjects in the  forearm and the other one actually in  the upper arm and how do you modulate  the current going through the electrode  in order to give that sense of touch we  actually think that's one of the most  interesting parts of this work that  we've done is that when we began  stimulating with the very regular  pattern of pulses people would feel the  sense of touching something but they  would describe it as a tingling as a  paresthesia if you will so like your  hand waking up in the morning that pins  and needles effect they liked it because  they felt their hand they actually were  very encouraged about that we wanted to  get a little more sophisticated and so  we've done is we begun varying the  strength just slightly and and what we  believe we're doing is that we are then  changing the the activation of different  axons in different ways within the hand  and so that information then is  translated to the brain it interprets it  as a more natural input as opposed to  this abnormal regular pulsing we have  placed electrodes around each of the  three main nerves that go to the hand  which are the median ulnar and radial  and so the median has the first three  digits at the hand the ulnar has the the  ring finger and pinky and the radial has  the back of the hand and so around each  of the nerves we've placed eight little  electrical points and when you turn  those on each of them gives us unique  location on the hand so what type of  tasks can the amputees now do with their  prosthetic limb which they couldn't do  before one that we we're pretty certain  of now are things like delicate tasks  for example we look at cherries when in  the nice thing about cherry even though  it's sort of visual is that if you  squeeze too hard it crushes if you don't  squeeze hard enough you can't pull the  stem out and so even with vision they  weren't able to perform that task  particularly well when we turn sensation  on they were able actually to do it  almost perfect every time so it makes a  big difference where they can do it the  other thing that they couldn't do before  which is very interesting is just feel  somebody else's hand and you know when  we asked our subjects what do you really  want to get you know functions great but  they want to hold for example their  spouses hand and when we would talk to  some of the the subjects or their  spouses they would say you know I don't  let them hold my hand because it hurts  and so now when they get the sensation  they feel touching something and so  that's what they've been looking for is  just that that interaction the human  interaction of touch again which is how  we interact communicate and explore the  world this is the first long-term study  2a shown sensation over I believe two  years right correct so that's why we're  really excited as well is that there's  been reports of other interfaces that  that have given sensation but they've  been short-term and this is the first  time it's been applied and showing that  where they feel the sensation has been  basically the same for the last two  years so we believe that this is a  long-term repeatable reliable interface  to the nervous system and apparently  this new technology also helps with  phantom limb pain which I believe is a  major problem for amputees yeah both of  our subjects came in and said that they  would have debilitating pain you know it  can imagine your hand one who described  it as a vise that would continue to  crush his hand until the fact he felt  the fingers were gonna go through his  palm they said keep going until they did  and then you can't you can't rub it  that's just not there so it's just pain  and so for us we didn't study pain per  se but both of our subjects have  reported that since we've done the  stimulation they've basically 90 95  percent or virtually pain-free they  don't have that any longer and they've  been very grateful for that and so what  are the next steps for you and your lab  and this fascinating new technology  right so the first electrodes we put in  we mentioned eight contacts around each  nerve that was that was just a low  number we wanted to get to see what we  could do so we believe because every one  of those was unique that we can begin to  increase that number of electrodes  around there and the way we do the  stimulation so we've taken these  stimulation patterns they go to touch  and we  seen that we can get more complex  sensations we're actually working with  colleagues to get really understand that  and take that to the next level where we  can maybe feel silk or sandpaper or  whatever they have to be touching and  then the last thing we're doing is this  has been in the lab and you know it's  really interesting one of our subjects  one time decided as he was leaving the  lab he says you know as he was taking  his process with him he says well I hate  leaving my hand here it's kind of  interesting the fact that he wasn't  going to feel his hand was what he's  referring to so even though he had a  tool the prosthesis he didn't feel like  he had his hands so we want to get to  the point where they can now take  sensation home with them system they can  use outside of the laboratory  environment and every day dr. Tyler  thank you so much for joining us today  and congratulations on a landmark paper  thank you
 

